# arc-unblocked.rq (loop the-arc-has-a-denominator, 2026-08-21) — WORK THAT IS READY AND IS
# NOT BEING DONE.
#
# The unmet criteria that NO register row is named as blocking (spec 2026-08-20 §5, third
# bullet: *"a criterion that nothing blocks any more, and is still unmet, is work that is
# ready and is not being done"*). This is the query the maintainer's original complaint most
# directly asks for: not "what is left" — arc-position.rq counts that — but "what is left
# that has no excuse".
#
# GATE CLASSIFICATION (CLAUDE.md §8): **AXIOM / derivation, OPEN WORLD, evidence-positive.**
# The row is grown from present triples — the rung, the criterion's key, its asserted
# prog:met false, its prog:statement. The output is a SELECTION of criteria, never a derived
# fact ABOUT one: nothing here says a criterion is met, ready, easy, or next. It writes
# nothing and constructs nothing (spec §9 — code validates the hand-authored graph and never
# writes it).
#
# THE ONE NEGATION, AND ITS SCOPE. `FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?criterion prog:blockedBy ?r }` is
# query-local and HOLON-SCOPED in the strict sense: it closes over ONE CRITERION — the same
# node the surrounding pattern already bound — and asks whether THAT node carries an edge.
# It closes nothing about the rung, the register, or the graph. Another criterion's edges are
# invisible to it, and a residue's silence is never read as a fact. That is exactly the
# closure CLAUDE.md §8 permits ("query-local NOT EXISTS closes WITHIN the one holon while the
# graph stays open") and exactly the closure it forbids elsewhere: this query never concludes
# `met` from an absence — met is read, never derived.
#
# THE HALF OF THE SPEC'S SENTENCE THIS QUERY CANNOT SEE, stated rather than quietly dropped.
# §5 says *"criteria whose every prog:blockedBy row is now CLOSED"*. Closure is a fact about
# docs/superpowers/residues.md — a markdown file that is not in the graph at all, which is
# why M7 reads it in procedural code (tests/test_arc_manifest.py, module docstring). So this
# query implements the reading the graph can support: *unmet, and nothing is named as
# blocking it*. The two readings are related by measurement, not by assumption:
#
#   MEASURED 2026-08-21 on tests/arc-manifest.ttl x docs/superpowers/residues.md — all 15
#   distinct residues reachable from prog:blockedBy are `open`. So the set "criteria all of
#   whose blockers have since closed" is EMPTY today, and this query's 17 rows are the whole
#   honest answer to "what is ready".
#
# The residual case is worth naming because it is the one direction this query errs in: when
# a residue closes and its edge is left in place, the criterion it named stops appearing here
# until a hand removes the stale edge. The error is towards UNDER-reporting readiness — it
# never claims work is ready when it is not — and it is visible from the other side, because
# arc-frontier.rq goes on listing a closed residue as a frontier. Nothing in the membrane
# refuses a prog:blockedBy naming a CLOSED row today (M7 checks presence in the index, not
# state); that refusal is a candidate for a later loop, not a silent assumption here.
#
# MEASURED against tests/arc-manifest.ttl, 2026-08-21: 17 rows — dec 6, etkl 6, holon 2,
# substrate 3, tab 0. Read it beside arc-position.rq's 26 unmet (43 declared - 17 met): 17 of those unmet criteria
# have nothing named as blocking them, and every one of the 9 that does is on `tab`.
PREFIX prog: <https://w3id.org/iladub/progress#>

SELECT DISTINCT ?rungKey ?criterion ?statement WHERE {
  # The rung, positively — same reason as arc-position.rq; and DISTINCT for the same reason
  # as arc-frontier.rq's, which is that the join is what a duplicated prog:Rung node doubles.
  # The membrane admits two rung nodes sharing one prog:rungKey (M6 constrains the value, not
  # the number of nodes carrying it — measured, `Conforms: True`), so a derivation handed an
  # unvalidated graph must not report one ready criterion as two pieces of work.
  ?rung a prog:Rung ;
        prog:rungKey ?rungKey .
  ?criterion a prog:Criterion ;
             prog:ofRung ?rungKey ;
             prog:met false ;
             prog:statement ?statement .
  # Holon-scoped closure over this one criterion (see the header). ?r is existentially
  # quantified inside the NOT EXISTS and is deliberately not projected: the question is
  # whether ANY blocking edge is named, and naming which one is arc-frontier.rq's job.
  FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?criterion prog:blockedBy ?r }
}
ORDER BY ?rungKey ?criterion
